Typed / validated properties

All property values are free-text strings, however it might be valuable to
support user-defined restrictions and transformations on these values. One
potentially option is to support an embedded scripting language (or just shell
scripts in a folder in the namespace tree) that are analogous to git hooks that
can be invoked when a property is set/changed/removed and have the opportunity
to "veto" or error the operation or transfer the value. For example, this could
be used to enforce that property a is always set to a single value that is an
internal link, and reject set operations that do not validate. Another use case
is to enforce an ordering to the values of a property, such as lexical sorting.

For a first pass of this, we should add a folder to the namespace tree called
prop-hooks with subfolders on-create, on-update, on-delete. The folders are
searched for an executable file/script of the name of the property being
updated. These scripts expect the value(s) to be written to stdin and to write
the resulting values to stdout. If the operation should fail, a non-0 exit code
is returned. These scripts are *not* executed for "protected" properties, as the
lifecycle and values for those are maintained by the tsk command itself. Example
scripts should be supplied, but not marked executable (the tool should ignore
these).